Let's learn the four most important data structures in simple langauge, with examples, real-life comparisons, and practice questions.

The four data structures are List, Dictionary, Set, Tuples.

List [] --> A collection of items that is ordered and changeable. Duplicate values are allowed.
Example: A list of your subjects
Tuple () --> A ordered collection that cannot be changed after creation. Duplicate values are allowed.
Example: Fixed GPS coordinates
Dictionary {} --> Stores information as key-value pairs. 
Example: A student record containing name, age and marks.
Set () --> A collection of unique elements. Duplicate values are automatically removed.
Example: Keeping only unique student IDs.

1. Understand the difference between List, Tuple, Dictionary, Set.
Solution. 

Feature      List        Tuple       Dictionary               Set 
Syntax       []           {}           {key: value}           {} or set()
ordered      Yes          Yes        Yes, Insertion order     No guaranteed order
Changeable   Yes          No             Yes                      Yes 
Duplicates    Yes          Yes       Keys: no, values: yes      No
Access by index Yes       Yes           Yes by keys               No
Main purpose Store sequences  Fixed Sequences Maps keysto values    Unique items 

IMPORTANT POINT TO REMEMBER --> Lists are flexible, tuples are fixed, dictionaries connect keys to values and sets remove duplicates.

2. What is a List ?
Solution. A list store multiple values in a single variable.

Code (WITHOUT THE LIST):

subject1 = "Python"
subject2 = "Maths"
subject3 = "Statistics"

Code (WITH THE HELP OF LIST):

subjects = ["Python", "Maths", "Statistics"]
print(subjects)

OUTPUT of the Code:

['Python' ,'Maths', 'Statistics']

IMPORTANT POINT - A list can contain integers, strings, decimal numbers, Boolean values, and even other lists.

Code:

data = [10, "Python", 3.14, True]
print(data)

Output of the Code is same as above. Remember --> List can contains another lists.

2.1 Indexing: Accesing list elements 
Python uses zero-based indexing, meaning the first element has index 0.
The index tells python which position you want.

Code:

languages = ["Python", "Java", "C++", "Javascript"]
print(languages[0]) #Python
print(languages[1]) #Java
print(languages[-1]) #Javascript
print(languages[-2]) #C++

Important point - Negative indexing starts from the end: -1 is the last element.

2.2 Slicing: extracting a portion of the list 

syntax:

Code:

list_name[start:stop:step]

***Important point -> The Stop index is excluded.

Code:

numbers=[10,20,30,40,50,60]
print(numbers[1:4])
print(numbers[:3])
print(numbers[3:])
print(numbers[::2])
print(numbers[::-1])

IMPORTANT POINT -> Slicing is especially useful when you want to extract data without changing the original list.

Output of the aboev code:

[20,30,40]
[10,20,30]
[40,50,60]
[10,30,50]
[60,50,40,30,20,10]

